#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198180 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198282 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0809c075-e1be-5d49-b1fe-5433114bcf16" :knowledge-point/title "\"If-Then\", \"If\", \"Only If\", and \"Implies\""}] :question/id #uuid "d20f2ac9-3190-5c58-a0a5-ddcd8c3f5817" :question/math-academy-id "e-13234" :question/problem "Consider the following predicates:\n\n$\\qquad$ $P(x)$: $x$ is prime $\\qquad$ $Q(x)$: $x$ is not a multiple of $10$\n\nWhich of the following expresses the conditional predicate $P(x) \\Rightarrow Q(x)$?\n\n1. $x$ is not a multiple of $10$ if $x$ is prime\n2. If $x$ is not a multiple of $10$, then $x$ is prime\n3. $x$ is prime implies $x$ is not a multiple of $10$\n4. $x$ is not a multiple of $10$ only if $x$ is prime" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the following grammatical constructions for the implication $P\\Rightarrow Q{:}$\n\n- If $P$, then $Q$\n- $P$ only if $Q$\n- $Q$ if $P$\n- $P$ implies $Q$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Option I reads\n$x$\nwhich corresponds to $P(x) \\Rightarrow Q(x)$. $\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n- Option II reads\n$x$\nwhich corresponds to $Q(x) \\Rightarrow P(x)$. $\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$\n- Option III reads\n$x$\nwhich corresponds to $P(x) \\Rightarrow Q(x)$. $\\:{\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}$\n- Option IV reads\n$x$\nwhich corresponds to $Q(x) \\Rightarrow P(x)$. $\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198185 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198285 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c614609-dae7-5cde-a26a-52104d47ebf5" :knowledge-point/title "Biconditionals"}] :question/id #uuid "f4b354bf-6629-5574-9674-fdc3ca707b0a" :question/math-academy-id "e-13273" :question/problem "Express $P \\Leftrightarrow Q$ as a sentence given the following:\n\n- $P$: $A \\cup B = A$.\n- $Q$: $B \\subseteq A$." :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the following grammatical constructions for the biconditional $P\\Leftrightarrow Q{:}$\n\n- $P$ if and only if $Q$\n- $P$ iff $Q$\n- $P$ is necessary and sufficient for $Q$\n- $P$ is equivalent to $Q$\n- If $P$ then $Q$, and conversely\n\nTherefore, some correct grammatical constructions of $P \\Leftrightarrow Q$ are the following:\n\n- $A \\cup B = A$ **if and only if** $B \\subseteq A$.\n- $A \\cup B = A$ **iff** $B \\subseteq A$.\n- $A \\cup B = A$ **is necessary and sufficient for** $B \\subseteq A$.\n- $A \\cup B = A$ **is equivalent to** $B \\subseteq A$.\n- **If** $A \\cup B = A$, **then** $B \\subseteq A$, **and conversely**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198187 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198283 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c26aeaa-9252-5bd3-9ad6-0fc25ca6b03c" :knowledge-point/title "Necessary and Sufficient Conditions"}] :question/id #uuid "8ba22130-b8bb-55d0-83b0-5a3b89bcf876" :question/math-academy-id "e-13275" :question/problem "Complete the following table:\n\n| Sentence | Symbolic Form |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $B$ is sufficient for $A$ |  |\n| For $A$, it is sufficient that $B$ |  |\n| $B$ is necessary for $A$ |  |\n| For $A$, it is necessary that $B$ |  |" :question/worked-solution "Given that the implication $S \\Rightarrow N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N$.\n- $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S$.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\textbf{S}\\textrm{ufficient} \\: \\Rightarrow \\: \\textbf{N}\\textrm{ecessary}\n$$\n\nWe have the following grammatical constructions for $S\\Rightarrow N{:}$\n\n- $S$ is sufficient for $N$\n- For $N$, it is sufficient that $S$\n- $N$ is necessary for $S$\n- For $S$, it is necessary that $N$\n\nLet's now compare these against the entries in our table:\n\n- \"$B$ is sufficient for $A$\" means $B\\Rightarrow A$.\n- \"For $A$, it is sufficient that $B$\" means $B\\Rightarrow A$.\n- \"$B$ is necessary for $A$\" means $A\\Rightarrow B$.\n- \"For $A$, it is necessary that $B$\" means $A\\Rightarrow B$.\n\nThe completed table is shown below.\n\n| Sentence | Symbolic Form |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $B$ is sufficient for $A$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}B\\Rightarrow A}$ |\n| For $A$, it is sufficient that $B$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}B\\Rightarrow A}$ |\n| $B$ is necessary for $A$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}A\\Rightarrow B}$ |\n| For $A$, it is necessary that $B$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}A\\Rightarrow B}$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198188 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198284 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b24ae815-5706-5b65-bab8-2d67f8405c9c" :knowledge-point/title "\"Whenever\", and \"Provided That\""}] :question/id #uuid "c776807d-efab-534b-b79c-582e4906bb4b" :question/math-academy-id "e-13276" :question/problem "Consider the following predicates:\n\n$\\qquad$ $P(x)$: $x\\in\\mathbb {Z}^{-}$ $\\qquad$ $Q(x)$: $x$ is negative\n\nWhich of the following expresses the conditional predicate $P(x) \\Rightarrow Q(x)$?\n\n1. Whenever $x$ is negative, then $x\\in\\mathbb {Z}^{-}$\n2. $x$ is negative whenever $x\\in\\mathbb {Z}^{-}$\n3. $x$ is negative, provided that $x\\in\\mathbb {Z}^{-}$" :question/worked-solution "Given that the implication $P \\Rightarrow Q$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- Whenever $P$, then $Q$\n- $Q$ whenever $P$\n- $Q$, provided that $P$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Option I reads\n$x$\nwhich corresponds to $Q(x) \\Rightarrow P(x)$. $\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$\n- Option II reads\n$x$\nwhich corresponds to $P(x) \\Rightarrow Q(x)$. $\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n- Option III reads\n$x$\nwhich corresponds to $P(x) \\Rightarrow Q(x)$. $\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198699 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198285 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c614609-dae7-5cde-a26a-52104d47ebf5" :kn